{"word": "fag", "source": "freedictionaryapi", "data": {"word": "fag", "entries": [{"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈfæɡ/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈfæɡ]", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈfeɪ̯ɡ/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈfeɪ̯ɡ]", "tags": []}], "forms": [{"word": "fags", "tags": ["plural"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(US, technical) In textile inspections, a rough or coarse defect in the woven fabric.", "tags": ["US"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(UK, Ireland, colloquial) A cigarette.", "tags": ["Ireland", "UK", "colloquial"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "He′d Phase Out Fag Industry\nLos Angeles (UPI) - A UCLA professor has called for the phasing out of the cigarette industry by converting tobacco acres to other crops.", "reference": "1968 January 25, The Bulletin, Oregon:"}, {"text": "Oh, rent a flat above a shop / And cut your hair and get a job / And smoke some fags and play some pool / Pretend you never went to school", "reference": "1995, Pulp, “Common People”, in Different Class:"}, {"text": "All of them, like my mother, were heavy smokers, and after warming themselves by the fire, they would sit on the sofa and smoke, lobbing their wet fag ends into the fire.", "reference": "2001, Oliver Sacks, Uncle Tungsten: Memories of a Chemical Boyhood, Alfred A. Knopf, section 15:"}, {"text": "So I started off by asking the shearers if they minded if I took a belly off while they were having a fag. Then after a while they were asking me. They′d say, ‘Do yer wanta take over fer a bit while I have a fag?’ And then I got better and I′d finish the sheep and they′d say ‘Christ, I haven′t finished me bloody fag yet, yer may as well shear anotherie.’", "reference": "2011, Bill Marsh, “A Female on the Board”, in Great Australian Shearing Stories, unnumbered page:"}], "synonyms": ["bine", "blem", "bogie", "boagie", "butt", "cancerette", "cancer stick", "boon", "cig", "cigaret", "cigarette", "ciggy", "coffin nail", "cowboy killer", "darb", "dart", "death stick", "durben", "durrie", "durry", "fag", "gaf", "gasper", "gwaai", "lung dart", "neen", "oily rag", "paper cigar", "rig", "rolly", "quirly", "scag", "skyf", "snout", "smoke", "square", "stick", "stinky stick", "stoge", "stoag", "stogie", "stoagie", "straight", "straighter", "tab", "toby rib", "twiff", "vogue", "zig-zag"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(UK, Ireland, obsolete, colloquial) The worst part or end of a thing.", "tags": ["Ireland", "UK", "colloquial", "obsolete"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Fag, s. the worst part or end of anything.", "reference": "1788, William Perry, editor, The Royal standard English dictionary:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈfæɡ/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈfæɡ]", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈfeɪ̯ɡ/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈfeɪ̯ɡ]", "tags": []}], "forms": [{"word": "fags", "tags": ["plural"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(colloquial, now rare) A chore: an arduous and tiresome task.", "tags": ["Ireland", "UK", "archaic", "colloquial"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "We are sadly off in the country; not but what we have very good shops in Salisbury, but it is so far to go—eight miles is a long way; Mr. Allen says it is nine, measured nine; but I am sure it cannot be more than eight; and it is such a fag—I come back tired to death.", "reference": "1817, Jane Austen, Northanger Abbey:"}, {"text": "Keeping up appearances is a bit of a fag, I grant, but if a girl respects herself it’s up to her not to let herself get slack.", "reference": "1923, Agatha Christie, The Murder on the Links:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(education, historical, colloquial) A younger student acting as a servant for senior students (potentially in a sexual or abusive way).", "tags": ["Ireland", "UK", "colloquial", "historical"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "I had the character at ſchool of being the very beſt fag that ever came into it.", "reference": "1791, Richard Cumberland, The Observer, volume 4, page 67:"}, {"text": "“He was my fag at Eton,” Warrington said. “I ought to have licked him a little more.”", "reference": "1848 November – 1850 December, William Makepeace Thackeray, The History of Pendennis. […], volume (please specify |volume=I or II), London: Bradbury and Evans, […], published 1849–1850, →OCLC:"}, {"text": "A gang of fags was mobbing about by the notice-boards. They fell silent as he approached. He patted one of them on the head. ‘Pretty children,’ he sighed, digging into his waistcoat pocket and pulling out a handful of change. ‘Tonight you shall eat.’ Scattering the coins at their feet, he moved on.", "reference": "1991, Stephen Fry, The Liar, page 18:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "verb", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈfæɡ/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈfæɡ]", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈfeɪ̯ɡ/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈfeɪ̯ɡ]", "tags": []}], "forms": [{"word": "fags", "tags": ["present", "singular", "third-person"]}, {"word": "fagging", "tags": ["participle", "present"]}, {"word": "fagged", "tags": ["participle", "past"]}, {"word": "fagged", "tags": ["past"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(transitive, colloquial, used mainly in passive form, now rare) To make exhausted, tired out.", "tags": ["archaic", "colloquial", "transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(intransitive, colloquial, now rare) To droop; to tire.", "tags": ["archaic", "colloquial", "intransitive"], "examples": ["a. 1829, G. Mackenzie, Lives, quoted in 1829, \"Fag\", entry in The London Encyclopaedia: Or, Universal Dictionary, Volume 9, page 12,\nCreighton with-held his force 'till the Italian began to fag, and then brought him to the ground."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(intransitive, UK, Ireland, education, historical, colloquial) (of a younger student) To act as a servant for senior students in many British boarding schools. To be the servant in fagging.", "tags": ["Ireland", "UK", "colloquial", "historical", "intransitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive, UK, Ireland, education, historical, colloquial) To have (a younger student) act as a servant in this way, in fagging.", "tags": ["Ireland", "UK", "colloquial", "historical", "transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "It is everywhere observed that a liberated slave is apt to make a merciless master, and that boys who have been cruelly fagged at school are cruel faggers.", "reference": "1887, Francis Bacon, Richard Whately, Essays, page 63:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(intransitive, UK, Ireland, now rare) To work hard, especially on menial chores.", "tags": ["Ireland", "UK", "archaic", "intransitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "This state of things should have been to me a paradise of peace, accustomed as I was to a life of ceaseless reprimand and thankless fagging; but, in fact, my racked nerves were now in such a state that no calm could soothe, and no pleasure excite them agreeably.", "reference": "1847, Charlotte Brontë, chapter 1, in Jane Eyre, HTML edition:"}, {"text": "I walked about the streets where the best shops for ladies were, I haunted the Bazaar like an unquiet spirit, I fagged through the Park again and again, long after I was quite knocked up.", "reference": "1849 May – 1850 November, Charles Dickens, The Personal History of David Copperfield, London: Bradbury & Evans, […], published 1850, →OCLC:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈfæɡ/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈfæɡ]", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈfeɪ̯ɡ/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈfeɪ̯ɡ]", "tags": []}], "forms": [{"word": "fags", "tags": ["plural"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(US, Canada, vulgar, usually offensive, sometimes endearing) A homosexual man.", "tags": ["Canada", "US", "endearing", "offensive", "sometimes", "usually", "vulgar"], "examples": ["[1921 John Lind, The Female Impersonators (Historical Documentation of American Slang v. 1, A-G, edited by Jonathan E. Lighter (New York: Random House, 1994) page 716.\nAndrogynes known as “fairies,” “fags,” or “brownies.”]"], "quotes": [{"text": "In schizophrenics, however, the homosexual outlet is sooner or later ... ideas that strangers call them \"cs,\" \"fairy,\" \"woman,\" \"fag,\" \" fruit,\" etc.). ...", "reference": "1926, American Neurological Association with New York Neurological Association et al., Journal of nervous and mental disease, volume 94, page 467:"}, {"text": "When they pick out a set of clubs for him to rent, he is so indifferent and silent the freckled kid in charge stares at him as if he's a moron. The thought flits through his brain that Eccles is known as a fag and he has become the new pet.", "reference": "1960, John Updike, 'Rabbit, Run', page 111:"}, {"text": "A couple of days later, Trisha tells Madelyn there is a rumor going around that she's a fag.", "